Add --open-terminal-on-start to reveal the integrated Terminal panel by default

This adds a new `--open-terminal-on-start` CLI flag (and env-var-driven default
setter can be added in src/node/cli.ts if desired) that sets `defaultLayout` in
the workbench web configuration, using VS Code's existing web-embedding API
(the same mechanism vscode.dev/github.dev use to seed initial view state).

It only takes effect when a workspace/folder is actually being opened
(--default-folder or --default-workspace), and only on that workspace's
first-ever load (`defaultLayout` is a first-load-only signal upstream, keyed
off IStorageService.isNew for that workspace) -- so it never fights a user who
later closes the terminal themselves, and it does not affect the bare/no-folder
workbench landing page.

To test: run code-server with --open-terminal-on-start --default-folder <dir>
against a folder that has never been opened before and confirm the integrated
terminal panel is visible on first load; then close it, reload, and confirm it
stays closed (proving this doesn't force the layout on every load).

